00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:03.510 align:start position:0%
 
hello  in this video we'll talk about  necroptosis  which is a method of  cellular death  when we hear the term necroptosis two  things come in our mind first of all  necrosis  and apoptosis  both necrosis and apoptosis are ways of  cellular death  and necrotosis is a hybrid of these two  necroptosis is regulated  necrosis mediated by several death  receptors  so here are some key features of  necroptosis  unlike necrosis necroptosis is highly  regulated program cell death  unlike apoptosis necroptosis does not  involve caspase activation  but there are a lot of similarities with  necrosis like loss of atp swelling of  the cell generation of the ross and  ultimately leakage of the lysosomal  enzymes  but at this point we should understand  why cell  need different ways to die because  apoptosis is already a program format of  cellular depth  then why do cell need another program  death pathway  gasps activation is a hallmark of  apoptosis but we have to remember that  caspase activation is not seen in case  of necroptosis  when it comes to apoptosis it is  involved in several developmental  pathway because it has been seen that  several mutations in the  gasps cascade pathway leads to  developmental defect  whereas several mutations in the  necrophosis pathway didn't show a  dramatic developmental phenotype that  kinds of  point out that these  necrotic pathways might not be useful  for developmental progression  but necroptosis has been implicated in  pathology  such as  acute tissue damages myocardial  infarction stroke or  ischemia reperfusion injury  in case of apoptosis secretion of  cytokine is absent or very less  so  there is no immune activation in case of  apoptosis  whereas in case of necroptosis  inflammatory cytokine release is one of  the hallmark which leads to immune  activation  necroptosis is evolutionary conserved in  the vertebrate fire  and it turns out to be an additional  defense to pathogens let me explain how  there are many pathogen especially  viruses which can infect the cell and it  can block  gas phases so these viruses encode  specific  enzymes that can break gas phases or  they can block gas bases so obviously if  caspases are blocked these cells would  not be killed by the immune system  and that would give a green card to  these viruses to survive in these cells  but in this scenario  necroptosis ensures that cell death  happens so it's kind of like a fail-safe  mechanisms and it also makes it is  another mechanism which protects our  cells  from these kind of viruses  now let us talk about briefly about the  signaling pathway it turns out these  necroptosis pathway can be triggered by  several signaling mediators at receptor  level tlr3 or tlr4 pnf receptor  interferon receptor can trigger this  kind of necrotic signaling  now the downstream signaling molecules  are not well described they are  there are some  molecules which are well described and  characterized  such as  rib k1  and ribcage  iii both of them are kinases which are  involved in several aspects of the  necrophosis pathway  eventually another kinase known as mlk  l3 is  a important player  but whatever is downstream to all of  this is a  certain event such as lower production  of atp  generation of increased ross which can  eventually damage the lipid membrane and  lead to lipid peroxidation  dna damage and eventually leaking of the  lysosomal enzyme and thereby death of  the cell as a whole so in this video we  discussed the overall concept behind  crop ptosis and its implication in  developmental and development and  disease  so if you like this video give it a  quick thumbs up don't forget to support  me on patreon you can also support me on  bimupi app if you're an indian viewer  you can take my unacademic course and  use my code ap10 to get in person  discount don't forget to like share and  subscribe do let me know in the comment  if you have any suggestions or you want  any particular topic to  be  described in a video format so you can  always tell me thank you

